{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 3, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a medieval Latin Christian count who ruled the County of Tripoli from 1132 until his death in 1152. He was the son of Ponce II Count of Tripoli and Adela of Valence. Raymond III was the second son of Ponce II and held the title of count from a young age after the death of his brother Gormais in 1128.\n\n### *Early Life and Reign*\n\nRaymond III was born around 1109. He succeeded his brother Gormais as count of Tripoli in 1128. Upon his accession to the throne Raymond III worked to strengthen the county's defenses against the Muslim forces from Egypt and Syria. He also sought to expand the county's borders through military campaigns against the Fatimid Caliphate.\n\nRaymond III played a key role in the Third Crusade. He allied himself with King Baldwin III of Jerusalem and supported the crusading efforts. However his efforts were unsuccessful. The crusaders were unable to retake the city of Edessa which had been captured by the Muslim forces from the Zengid dynasty.\n\n### *Military Campaigns and Politics*\n\nDuring his reign Raymond III led several military campaigns. He fought against the Zengid dynasty and the Fatimid Caliphate. In 1148 Raymond III allied himself with King Louis VII of France and King Conrad III of Germany during the Second Crusade. However this alliance was unsuccessful and the crusaders were unable to achieve their goals.\n\nRaymond III also played a key role in the politics of the Latin East. He was a key member of the Council of Nablus which was convened in 1143 to discuss the affairs of the Latin East. The council was attended by representatives from several Latin kingdoms including the Kingdom of Jerusalem the County of Tripoli and the Principality of Antioch.\n\n### *Succession and Legacy*\n\nRaymond III died in 1152. He was succeeded by his nephew Raymond III's nephew Raymond IV who went on to rule the county of Tripoli for nearly 50 years. Raymond III's legacy is complex. On the one hand he played a key role in the politics of the Latin East and supported the Third Crusade. On the other hand his efforts were often unsuccessful and he was unable to achieve his goals.\n\nRaymond III is also remembered for his role in the founding of the Knights Hospitaller. He founded the first hospital in Tripoli which became a major center for the Knights Hospitaller. The Knights Hospitaller would go on to become a major military order in the Latin East.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0011754278093576431}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0001311216183239594}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.20655624568462372}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.802703940891661e-05}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.00026901919045485556}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -6.12716976320371e-05}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008780678734183311}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.36229461431503296}, {"token": " medieval", "logprob": -1.739331603050232}, {"token": " Latin", "logprob": -3.1739578247070312}, {"token": " Christian", "logprob": -0.7103630900382996}, {"token": " count", "logprob": -4.704780101776123}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.5089221000671387}, {"token": " ruled", "logprob": -0.10600537061691284}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.2823861837387085}, {"token": " County", "logprob": -0.022784234955906868}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -6.782778655178845e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -0.00010859376925509423}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.3734271228313446}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.2717112600803375}, {"token": "113", "logprob": -3.4158241748809814}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -2.0600123405456543}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.2986716628074646}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.006864536087960005}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -0.0008272086852230132}